WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.945
Relaxing the binary decisions allows fractional opening.

00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:11.835
At production two on A, the relaxation chooses y A equal to one third and pays only one third of its fixed cost.

00:00:11.835 --> 00:00:13.314
B remains fully open.

00:00:13.314 --> 00:00:20.147
The relaxed value is fifty two point six six seven, above the attainable integer value forty six.

00:00:20.147 --> 00:00:28.248
One third of an opened line is a mathematical device for bounding, not an allowable purchase or operating decision.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.401
For this comparison, retain the physical production limits six and eight.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:38.826
Only loosen the linking constants to one hundred.

00:00:38.826 --> 00:00:46.024
The relaxed model can then use y A equal to zero point zero two and y B equal to zero point zero eight.

00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:51.125
It earns sixty three point one six because it pays too little fixed cost.

00:00:51.125 --> 00:00:53.221
The binary model is unchanged.

00:00:53.221 --> 00:00:58.812
Without the separate physical limits, we would be comparing a different problem.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:08.463
Branch and bound maintains a feasible integer incumbent and optimistic bounds for regions that have not been eliminated.

00:01:08.463 --> 00:01:15.395
In a maximization problem, a node whose upper bound is no better than the incumbent cannot improve the answer.

00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:21.004
A node can also close because it is infeasible or its relaxed optimum is already integer.

00:01:21.004 --> 00:01:27.180
We will illustrate these ideas using the two batch variables, rather than a large industrial tree.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.104
Return to the batch model with resource limits four point five and six point five.

00:01:35.104 --> 00:01:38.848
Its root relaxation gives y equal to two point five.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:42.664
Any integer y must lie at most two or at least three.

00:01:42.664 --> 00:01:46.264
These disjoint branches lose no integer solutions.

00:01:46.264 --> 00:01:49.864
We keep all original constraints in both branches.

00:01:49.864 --> 00:01:55.768
Branching is not rounding one candidate; it partitions the remaining search space.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:05.725
In the left branch, the relaxed optimum becomes two point two five, two, with upper bound ten point seven five.

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:08.224
Now x is fractional, so split on x.

00:02:08.224 --> 00:02:13.650
The child with x at most two has the integer optimum two, two and value ten.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.435
This gives our incumbent.

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:23.432
Its feasibility can be checked directly, and every remaining node must beat ten to matter for the optimal value.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.553
The other child requires x at least three and y at most two.

00:02:29.553 --> 00:02:35.970
Its relaxed solution has x equals three and y equals zero point five, with objective value ten.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:41.374
Although that point is fractional, its upper bound already equals our incumbent.

00:02:41.374 --> 00:02:46.305
We can prune if our purpose is to find one optimum and certify the value.

00:02:46.305 --> 00:02:52.452
If we wanted every alternative optimum, equality pruning would need more careful treatment.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:02.465
The right branch initially has upper bound ten point five at one point five, three, so it cannot yet be discarded.

00:03:02.465 --> 00:03:04.072
Split x at one and two.

00:03:04.072 --> 00:03:08.474
The first child has bound ten and cannot improve the incumbent.

00:03:08.474 --> 00:03:16.229
The second requires x at least two and y at least three, forcing x plus y at least five, above four point five.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.884
No unexplored branch can now beat ten.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.132
The proof combines a feasible plan with bounds covering every alternative.

00:03:26.132 --> 00:03:27.971
Our incumbent reaches ten.

00:03:27.971 --> 00:03:33.062
Each branch either has no feasible point or has upper bound at most ten.

00:03:33.062 --> 00:03:36.526
Therefore no feasible integer plan can be better.

00:03:36.526 --> 00:03:40.627
This is a global certificate for the linear integer model.

00:03:40.627 --> 00:03:48.476
Modern solvers add presolve, cuts and heuristics, so their actual node counts need not match our teaching tree.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:56.820
A solver stopped by a time limit may have a useful feasible plan, or it may have none.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.759
Inspect status before reading variables.

00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:02.699
In our code we minimize negative profit.

00:04:02.699 --> 00:04:10.855
Therefore negative fun is the feasible profit, and negative mip dual bound is an upper bound on maximum profit.

00:04:10.855 --> 00:04:14.015
The field mip gap describes the solver gap.

00:04:14.015 --> 00:04:20.188
It is different from comparing the original L P relaxation with the integer optimum.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.640
This figure is a separate machine sizing example, not the two line plant.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:33.154
Demand is D, each machine supplies four units, and each machine costs ten.

00:04:33.154 --> 00:04:35.762
Producing each unit also costs two.

00:04:35.762 --> 00:04:42.245
For nonnegative demand, the required machine count is the ceiling of D divided by four.

00:04:42.245 --> 00:04:46.269
Total minimum cost is ten times that count plus two D.

00:04:46.269 --> 00:04:50.442
Within a fixed machine count, cost rises with slope two.

00:04:50.442 --> 00:04:54.838
Crossing capacity requires another machine and a cost jump.

00:04:54.838 --> 00:04:59.160
Do not extrapolate an L P shadow price across that switch.
